{
	title: 'Accept commands with read-only batch concurrency',
	spec: 'The native tool accepts multiple complete tool commands separated by top-level newlines, for any registered tools, in both provider calls and hal/tool direct mode. The native tool definition teaches one or more commands on separate lines and requires parallel calls for file reading and research. It explains that read-only tools run in parallel. HELP teaches one quoted COMMAND syntax with optional arguments and quotes the INSPECT subscriptions topic; structured INSPECT usage spaces the array and object delimiters. Commands in one call run concurrently only when every member is read-only. If any member has effects, the entire batch runs sequentially in written order, including reads before and after the effectful member. BASH, COMMAND and unknown tools are effectful; built-in retrieval tools and tools declaring readOnly are read-only. The prompt-amendment exemption for harmless slash commands does not make COMMAND read-only for scheduling. WAIT retains its ordering after other round work in all-read-only batches; effectful batches retain written order. This scheduling is shared by providers and hal/tool, terminal and web. Quoted strings, structured arguments and comments containing newlines or tool names are never split. Resolve all commands before dispatch so malformed or ambiguous input runs nothing. Each expanded command is durably recorded with its own result and normal approval, cancellation, background-job identity, unsafe-to-stop controls, file leases, complete errors and output. Expanded commands replay as ordinary native tool calls with their matching results. Runtime failures remain visible and later commands retain normal execution unless canceled or declined. Separate native calls retain existing concurrency and WAIT ordering. Invariant: clear requests must not fail solely because multiple complete commands share one native call. Regression cases include repeated BASH commands, multiple EDIT commands and mixed tools, all-read-only concurrency, whole-batch sequential ordering with an effectful member, WAIT ordering, malformed trailing calls, multiline argument boundaries and approval.',
	status: 'done',
	notes: [
		'Commands expand before history recording into ordinary tool-call blocks with distinct IDs and a durable sequence ID. This keeps each approval, result, background-job target and cancellation on the existing path; replay sends the expanded native calls and matching results. Sequential EDITs are excluded from same-round coalescing so later edits still obey their leases. Runtime failures remain individual results; following commands still execute unless the normal cancellation or approval path prevents them. The scheduler lives in tools.round to keep turns.ts under the source limit. Parsing and scheduling hold only per-call/per-round state; single-line input bypasses compound scanning.',
		'Verification: 44 focused tests passed across tool-sequence, native-tool, tool-model, turns, turns-approval, turns-stop, jobs and work. The normal suite ran 1578 tests: 1568 passed and 10 failed; baseline comparison is recorded separately. Lint reported zero warnings and errors.',
		'Unchanged HEAD 7d4c39f5 also failed its normal suite: 1563 passed, 11 failed, including the same cleanup, rebase, neighbor ordering, server signal, source-size, lazy-history and render failures, plus prompt timeouts. The baseline checkout location introduced two additional file-path test failures. Focused baseline prompt tests passed, while full-suite prompt failures varied; those suite interactions are not a clean verification signal. No unrelated failing tests were changed.',
		'User decision (session 13-gar): concurrency is whole-batch, not read groups separated by effects. The scheduler splits wholly read-only compound groups into ordinary dispatch units; mixed groups retain their durable sequence and exclude EDIT coalescing. COMMAND is excluded from scheduling read-only status even when prompt amendment treats rename/help as harmless. WAIT members of all-read-only groups use the existing after-other-work dispatch. Provider and hal/tool concurrency, ordered results, effectful and unknown calls, and WAIT ordering passed in 33 focused tests.',
		'After integrating shared changes, 34 focused tests pass across tool-sequence, native-tool, tool-model, turns-approval, turns-stop and work. The source-only TypeScript check and lint of changed modules pass. The existing direct-mode test now waits for the durable model-change event rather than greeting copy, restoring the invariant that tool execution checks do not depend on wording. Scheduling tests retain host/provider replay and result-order contracts and use gates rather than elapsed-time assertions. Scheduling adds only per-round arrays, with no persistent state or timers.'
